<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dublin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-liffey">River Liffey</a> on the east coast. Founded as a Norse settlement in the 9th century, it became the centre of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> kingdom and, after the Anglo-Norman invasion of 1169–1171, the seat of English government in Ireland. It was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916 and became the capital of the independent Irish state in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dublin.jpg" alt="Dublin" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dublin_-_aerial_-_2025-07-07_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dublin lies at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-liffey">River Liffey</a> in east-central Ireland, and its urban area covers about 345 square kilometres. The Dublin Mountains, a low sub-range of the Wicklow Mountains, border it on the south, with flat farmland to the north and west. The Liffey divides the city into the Northside and the Southside, and the city itself was founded where the River Poddle met the Liffey. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> on the southside and the Royal Canal on the northside ring the inner city on their way from the west, both connecting with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a>.</p><p>The name comes from the Middle Irish Du(i)blind, literally Blackpool, from dubh, meaning black, and linn, meaning pool. It referred to a dark tidal pool on the site of the castle gardens behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>, where the Poddle entered the Liffey. Old Norse Dyflin, Welsh Dulyn and Breton Dulenn all preserve the Middle Irish pronunciation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fish traps found during construction of the Convention Centre indicate human habitation 6,000 years ago, and others near St. James's Gate show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> activity. Ptolemy's map of about 140 CE gives what may be the earliest reference to a settlement nearby, calling it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eblana">Eblana</a> polis. A Christian ecclesiastical settlement known as Duibhlinn preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlement of about 841, Dyflin, which took its name from it; an excavation in Temple Bar has also found evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> there before 841. A Gaelic settlement, Áth Cliath, 'ford of hurdles', stood further up the river. The Irish government recognised 988 as the year of the city's first settlement, and Dublin marked its official millennium in 1988.</p><p>As the Kingdom of Dublin grew, the city became Ireland's principal settlement by the 12th century, following the Anglo-Norman invasion. It expanded rapidly from the 17th century and, after the Acts of Union in 1800, was briefly the second-largest city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and sixth largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Trinity College was established by Royal Charter in 1592 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, and Guinness has been brewed at the St. James's Gate Brewery since 1759. After independence in 1922 Dublin became the capital of the Irish Free State, renamed Ireland in 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland and its economic centre. The historic centre lies between the two canals, with O'Connell Street as its main thoroughfare and Grafton Street and Henry Street as the main shopping streets. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-dublin">University of Dublin</a>, the oldest university in Ireland, has Trinity College as its sole constituent college; the college was closed to Roman Catholics until 1793. Dublin City Council is led by a Lord Mayor, elected for a yearly term, who lives in the Mansion House.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dublin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He styled himself king of the English, and in 937 he defeated a coalition of Scots, Strathclyde Britons and <b>Dublin</b> Vikings at the Battle of Brunanburh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Acts of Union of 1800, passed by the British and Irish parliaments after extensive use of patronage in <b>Dublin</b>, abolished the Irish Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade was modest: the kingdom exported cattle, hides and wool and imported wine, salt and cloth, much of it through contacts across the Irish Sea, especially with Hiberno-Norse <b>Dublin</b>, where Gruffudd ap Cynan was born and raised.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Handel’s Messiah had its first performance not in London but in <b>Dublin</b>, at the Great Music Hall on Fishamble Street in April 1742.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ireland had been a separate kingdom under the English, then British, crown, with a Parliament in <b>Dublin</b> confined to Protestants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…across a vast area: to England and Ireland, where they founded kingdoms and towns such as York and <b>Dublin</b>; to Normandy; to the Faroe Islands, Iceland, settled from around 870, and Greenland; along the rivers of Russia and Ukraine to Constantinople and the Caspian Sea; and briefly to…</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
